 {"id": "g5", "t": "Nominalisation (academic style)", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Academic writing often turns verbs and adjectives into nouns. This lets you pack more information into a sentence and sound more objective: <i>Prices increased rapidly</i> → <i>There was a rapid increase in prices</i>.", "ex": ["The government decided to … → The government's decision to …", "Unemployment rose sharply. → There was a sharp rise in unemployment.", "People are becoming more aware of … → There is growing awareness of …", "Because the company failed … → The failure of the company …"], "q": [["Sales fell dramatically in 2020. (There was …)", "There was a dramatic fall in sales in 2020."], ["The city expanded quickly, which caused problems. (The rapid …)", "The rapid expansion of the city caused problems."], ["Scientists discovered the virus, which changed medicine. (The discovery …)", "The discovery of the virus changed medicine."]]},
 {"id": "g6", "t": "Hedging and cautious language", "lvl": "C1", "body": "In academic and IELTS writing, avoid over-strong claims. Use modal verbs (<b>may, might, could</b>), verbs (<b>appear, seem, tend, suggest</b>), adverbs (<b>possibly, arguably, largely</b>) and phrases (<b>to some extent, in most cases</b>).", "ex": ["Social media causes depression. → Heavy social media use may contribute to depression.", "This proves that … → This suggests that …", "Everyone agrees … → It is widely accepted that …", "It is arguably the most important factor."], "q": [["Video games make children violent. (hedge it)", "Some violent video games may make some children more aggressive."], ["This shows the policy failed. (hedge it)", "This appears to suggest that the policy was largely unsuccessful."]]},
 {"id": "g7", "t": "Advanced passives", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Use reporting passives to sound impersonal: <b>It is said/believed/thought that …</b> or <b>Subject + is said/believed + to + infinitive</b> (<b>to have + past participle</b> for the past). Also <b>have/get something done</b>.", "ex": ["It is believed that the painting is a fake.", "The painting is believed to be a fake.", "He is thought to have left the country.", "The company is reported to be losing money.", "We're having the kitchen redone."], "q": [["People say that he is a millionaire. (He is said …)", "He is said to be a millionaire."], ["People believe the fire started in the kitchen. (The fire is …)", "The fire is believed to have started in the kitchen."], ["Someone is repairing my car. (I'm having …)", "I'm having my car repaired."]]},
 {"id": "g8", "t": "The subjunctive and 'unreal' past", "lvl": "C2", "body": "After verbs and adjectives of demand or importance, formal English uses the base form for all persons: <b>insist/recommend/suggest/essential that he be / go</b>. After <b>wish, if only, it's (high) time, would rather</b>, use a past form for the present.", "ex": ["The committee recommended that she be promoted.", "It is essential that every student attend the meeting.", "I'd rather you didn't tell anyone.", "It's high time we left.", "If only I had more time."], "q": [["The doctor said he must rest. (The doctor insisted that he …)", "The doctor insisted that he rest."], ["We should go now. (It's time …)", "It's time we went."], ["Please don't smoke here. (I'd rather you …)", "I'd rather you didn't smoke here."]]},
 {"id": "g9", "t": "Ellipsis and substitution", "lvl": "C2", "body": "Fluent speakers leave out or replace words that are already clear: <b>so / not</b> (I think so / I hope not), <b>do so</b>, <b>one / ones</b>, and leaving out a verb after an auxiliary (She can swim but I can't).", "ex": ["'Will it rain?' 'I hope not.'", "He promised to help and he did so.", "I wanted the red one, not the blue one.", "She's been to Japan, but I haven't.", "I'd like to come, but I'm not sure I'll be able to."], "q": [["'Is the shop open?' 'I think that it is open.' (shorten)", "'Is the shop open?' 'I think so.'"], ["He said he would call and he called. (do so)", "He said he would call and he did so."]]},
 {"id": "g10", "t": "Fronting and complex noun phrases", "lvl": "C2", "body": "Move information to the front for emphasis or to link with the previous sentence (<b>Gone are the days when …</b>, <b>Such was the demand that …</b>, <b>So severe was the storm that …</b>). Build long noun phrases with pre- and post-modifiers instead of several short sentences.", "ex": ["Gone are the days when a degree guaranteed a job.", "Such was the demand that tickets sold out in minutes.", "So severe was the storm that the airport closed.", "A recently published government report on youth unemployment in coastal towns …"], "q": [["The storm was so strong that trees fell. (So strong …)", "So strong was the storm that trees fell."], ["The demand was so great that the site crashed. (Such …)", "Such was the demand that the site crashed."]]},
 {"id": "g11", "t": "Linking: contrast and concession", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Know the grammar of each linker. <b>Although / even though / whereas / while</b> + clause. <b>Despite / in spite of</b> + noun or -ing. <b>However / nevertheless / nonetheless</b> start a new sentence (with a comma). <b>Albeit</b> + adjective or phrase (formal).", "ex": ["Although it was expensive, we bought it.", "Despite the cost, we bought it. / Despite being expensive, …", "It was expensive. Nevertheless, we bought it.", "The results were positive, albeit limited.", "Some prefer cities, whereas others prefer the countryside."], "q": [["It rained, but we played. (Despite …)", "Despite the rain, we played."], ["He is rich, but he is unhappy. (Although …)", "Although he is rich, he is unhappy."], ["The plan was successful but expensive. (albeit)", "The plan was successful, albeit expensive."]]},
